{"name":"Volcanic","hex":"#a55749","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"volcanic","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or pertaining to a volcano or volcanoes.","Produced by a volcano, or, more generally, by igneous agencies."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A volcanic rock."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/volcanic"}],"entries":[{"title":"Volcano","blocks":[{"text":"A volcano is a vent or fissure in the crust of a planetary-mass object that allows hot lava, volcanic ash, and gases to escape from a magma chamber below the surface. On Earth, volcanoes are most often found where tectonic plates are diverging or converging, and because most of Earth's plate boundaries are underwater, most volcanoes are found underwater. For example, a mid-ocean ridge, such as the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, has volcanoes caused by divergent tectonic plates whereas the Pacific Ring of Fire has volcanoes caused by convergent tectonic plates. Volcanoes resulting from divergent tectonic activity are usually non-explosive whereas those resulting from convergent tectonic activity cause violent eruptions. Volcanoes can also form where there is stretching and thinning of the crust's plates, such as in the East African Rift, the Wells Gray-Clearwater volcanic field, and the Rio Grande rift in North America. Volcanism away from plate boundaries most likely arises from upwelling diapirs from the core–mantle boundary called mantle plumes, 3,000 kilometres (1,900 mi) deep within Earth. This results in hotspot volcanism or intraplate volcanism, in which the plume may cause thinning of the crust and result in a volcanic island chain due to the continuous movement of the tectonic plate, of which the Hawaiian hotspot is an example. Volcanoes are usually not created at transform tectonic boundaries where two tectonic plates slide past one another."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Augustine_volcano_Jan_24_2006_-_Cyrus_Read.jpg/330px-Augustine_volcano_Jan_24_2006_-_Cyrus_Read.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Volcano"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Lakagigar_Iceland_2004-07-01.jpg/500px-Lakagigar_Iceland_2004-07-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Lakagigar_Iceland_2004-07-01.jpg/500px-Lakagigar_Iceland_2004-07-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Lakagigar fissure vent in Iceland, the source of the major world climate alteration of 1783–84, has a chain of volcanic cones along its length.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALakagigar_Iceland_2004-07-01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Skjaldbreidur_Herbst_2004.jpg/500px-Skjaldbreidur_Herbst_2004.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Skjaldbreidur_Herbst_2004.jpg/500px-Skjaldbreidur_Herbst_2004.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Skjaldbreiður, a shield volcano whose name means \"broad shield\"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASkjaldbreidur_Herbst_2004.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/East_Dome_Mount_St._Helens.jpg/500px-East_Dome_Mount_St._Helens.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/East_Dome_Mount_St._Helens.jpg/1280px-East_Dome_Mount_St._Helens.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/East_Dome_Mount_St._Helens.jpg/500px-East_Dome_Mount_St._Helens.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"East dome, a lava dome located on the lower east flank of St. Helens, part of the Sugar Bowl Eruptive Period (1800 YA).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEast_Dome_Mount_St._Helens.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Izalco_Volcano.jpg/500px-Izalco_Volcano.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Izalco_Volcano.jpg/500px-Izalco_Volcano.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Izalco volcano, the youngest volcano in El Salvador. Izalco erupted almost continuously from 1770 (when it formed) to 1958, earning it the nickname of \"Lighthouse of the Pacific\".","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIzalco_Volcano.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Mount_Vesuvius_Araucaria.jpg/500px-Mount_Vesuvius_Araucaria.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Mount_Vesuvius_Araucaria.jpg/1280px-Mount_Vesuvius_Araucaria.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Mount_Vesuvius_Araucaria.jpg/500px-Mount_Vesuvius_Araucaria.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mt. Vesuvius, a stratovolcano, Gulf of Naples.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMount_Vesuvius_Araucaria.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Lake_Taupo_%2831071970884%29.jpg/500px-Lake_Taupo_%2831071970884%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Lake_Taupo_%2831071970884%29.jpg/1280px-Lake_Taupo_%2831071970884%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https